## Why This Is Asked

This tests practical, beginner-friendly steps to mitigate a known-vulnerability in a gateway without full rewrite, emphasizing token lifecycle, key rotation, and dependency governance.

## Key Concepts

- JWT lifetimes and rotation
- Key management and revocation lists
- Gateway security posture
- Continuous integration security gates

## Code Example

```javascript
// Minimal token verification middleware (Express)
const jwt = require('jsonwebtoken');
const { getCurrentPublicKey, isRevoked } = require('./security');

function verifyToken(req, res, next) {
  const auth = req.headers.authorization || '';
  const token = auth.split(' ')[1];
  if (!token) return res.status(401).send('Unauthorized');
  try {
    const payload = jwt.verify(token, getCurrentPublicKey(), { algorithms: ['RS256'] });
    if (isRevoked(payload.jti)) return res.status(401).send('Revoked');
    req.user = payload;
    next();
  } catch {
    res.status(401).send('Unauthorized');
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you scale revocation checks across multiple gateway instances?
- What are the trade-offs of shorter token lifetimes vs user experience?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests a beginner's practical handling of untrusted input, safe logging, and data leakage risk in a common feature.

## Key Concepts

- JSON schema validation with no extraProperties
- Safe rendering with HTML escaping
- Logging hygiene and redaction
- Parameterized SQL queries and minimal data retention
- Basic test coverage for invalid input and log safety

## Code Example

```javascript
const Ajv = require("ajv");
const ajv = new Ajv();
const schema = {
  type: "object",
  properties: {
    message: { type: "string" },
    meta: { type: "object", properties: {}, additionalProperties: false }
  },
  required: ["message"],
  additionalProperties: false
};
if (!ajv.validate(schema, req.body)) {
  res.status(400).send("Invalid payload");
  return;
}
function sanitize(s){
  return String(s)
    .replace(/&/g, "&amp;")
    .replace(/</g, "&lt;")
    .replace(/>/g, "&gt;")
    .replace(/"/g, "&quot;")
    .replace(/'/g, "&#39;");
}
const safeMessage = sanitize(req.body.message);
db.query("INSERT INTO messages (message) VALUES ($1)", [safeMessage]);
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test for edge cases like extremely large JSON or nested payloads?
- How would you adapt this plan for real-time content rendering in a multi-tenant app?

